# Cross-platform line-ending policy (PLAN pre-mortem #6: Windows breakage).
# Shell scripts, bats tests, and YAML MUST be LF on checkout everywhere, or
# bash on Linux CI chokes on carriage returns. Authored on Windows Git Bash,
# executed on ubuntu CI -- LF is non-negotiable for anything a shell runs.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf
*.bats  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf

# Evidence bundles are sha256-hash-pinned (ADR-0002): git must NOT rewrite their
# bytes (line endings included) or tamper-verification breaks on checkout across
# platforms. Freeze them exactly as hashed -- this OVERRIDES `* text=auto eol=lf`.
docs/evidence/** -text

# Byte-exact hostile fixture for product-lint (Phase 00): the crlf-bom case tests
# BOM + CR rejection -- git must NOT normalize its bytes or the fixture silently
# stops testing what it claims. OVERRIDES `* text=auto eol=lf`.
tests/fixtures/products/hostile/crlf-bom/** -text

# Cycle 2 Phase 00: the spine's hostile corpus attacks the emitter at the BYTE level --
# CRLF, BOM, invalid UTF-8, raw control characters. Normalizing any of it would leave the
# fixture green while testing nothing. OVERRIDES `* text=auto eol=lf`.
tests/fixtures/spine/hostile/** -text

# Binary assets: never touch.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary

# The CRLF hostile fixture IS its bytes: the design-lint suite pins that a Windows-authored
# (CRLF) brief lints identically. Any eol conversion on add or checkout kills the pin.
tests/fixtures/design-lint/hostile/crlf.md -text

# Same reasoning, whole directory: develop-lint's adversarial fixtures are byte-level attacks.
# Two of them ARE their line endings, and git warned it would rewrite them on the next touch --
# which would have left the CRLF and mixed-ending cases byte-identical to the LF ones on CI,
# so the suite would have gone on reporting them CAUGHT while testing nothing.
tests/fixtures/develop/breaking/** -text

# Cycle 6, engine: process-lint's hostile corpus, same class again. `crlf.process.yaml` and
# `mixed-line-endings.process.yaml` ARE their line endings -- git warned it would rewrite them
# on the next touch, and it had already stored both with LF, so the two fixtures were green
# and testing nothing. Fifth occurrence of this shape in this file; the rule is now: any
# fixture directory whose cases attack at the byte level gets `-text` in the same commit that
# creates it. OVERRIDES `* text=auto eol=lf`.
tests/fixtures/engine/hostile/** -text

# Cycle 8, leads Phase 02: reply fixtures. SIXTH occurrence, and this one is worse than the
# five above -- here the bytes are not merely what the parser is attacked with, they ARE the
# receipt's identity. ADR-0414 keys `outreach.replied` on sha256 of the raw bytes, so any eol
# rewrite gives the same reply a different id on the Windows leg than on the ubuntu one, and
# the idempotency the whole ADR exists to provide silently stops holding across platforms.
# The corpus also carries deliberate CRLF and bare-LF cases whose whole point is the ending.
# Applied in the same commit that creates the directory, per the rule above.
tests/fixtures/leads/replies/** -text
tests/fixtures/leads/replies-hostile/** -text

# Cycle: ledger Phase 00. SEVENTH occurrence of the byte-level-fixture class, and this one was
# caught by git warning on the very commit that created the directory -- it had already stored
# `02-crlf-bom.csv` with 0 CRs against 3 in the working copy, so the CRLF-and-BOM case was
# byte-identical to the plain one and the parser suite would have reported it HANDLED while
# testing nothing. The export parsers are attacked at the byte level (CRLF, BOM, quoted commas),
# so the whole directory is frozen as authored. OVERRIDES `* text=auto eol=lf`.
tests/fixtures/ledger/** -text
